      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the low murmur of voices and the clink of cups. Outside, the morning air bites clean and cold against your face. The street is damp, the cobbles still dark from last night's rain.\n\nThe Ash Gate stands maybe sixty paces ahead, at the far end of a short, crooked lane. You can see it clearly now \u2014 a cracked black arch, bone-dry despite the wet morning, rising alone against a sky the colour of old pewter. No wall flanks it. No building leans against it. It simply *stands*, a door to nowhere set in open air.\n\nAs you approach, the ground underfoot changes. The cobbles give way to packed earth, then to bare stone worn smooth by countless crossings. The arch looms \u2014 fifteen feet high, ten across. Its surface is not carved stone but something denser, darker, like obsidian shot through with veins of dull iron. Up close, you can see the seal: a disc of pale wax set into a recess at the arch's keystone, stamped with a crest you don't recognize \u2014 a bird in flight, wings spread, clutching a key.\n\nThe air around the gate is still. Colder than it should be. No birds call nearby.\n\nAt the base of the arch, half-hidden in shadow, a shape lies crumpled against the stone.\n\nIt's a body.\n\nA courier's cloak, travel-stained and mud-caked, pooled around still shoulders. A satchel strap, snapped. One hand visible, pale and slack against the dark stone, fingers slightly curled.\n\nThe courier doesn't move."
    },
