      "narrative": "You step closer to the Ash Gate. The cold radiates from it \u2014 not the sharp bite of winter air, but something deeper, like heat bleeding *out* instead of in. You stop a few feet from the body, raise your voice, and call:\n\n\"Old Bram!\"\n\nThe name echoes off the stone. It bounces once against the arch's curve, then fades into the grey morning.\n\nSilence.\n\nNo voice answers from the gate. No shuffling footsteps. No groan of old bones stirring in the dark.\n\nThe courier's body does not move. The pale wax seal at the keystone holds fast.\n\nBut you notice something your ears catch, just at the edge of hearing \u2014 a sound so faint you almost convince yourself it was your imagination. A whisper, maybe. Paper rustling. Or the faintest trickle of something dry, like sand running through a narrow throat.\n\nIt came from *inside* the arch. Not from beyond it \u2014 from the stone itself.\n\nThen it stops. The silence returns, thicker than before. The morning holds its breath.\n\nThe courier's satchel strap lies snapped across the dead messenger's chest. One corner of a folded letter peeks from the torn leather \u2014 cream-coloured paper, smudged with dirt."
    },
