      "narrative": "You crouch low beneath the arch, your fingers brushing the cold stone ground. The dirt here is fine and grey \u2014 almost ash-like, as if the gate itself sheds its own dust. You sweep your hand across the surface, feeling for anything the eye might miss.\n\nNothing obvious. No hidden catch, no seam in the stone, no buried object.\n\nBut as your hand passes near the courier's boot, your fingers brush against something stiff tucked inside the leather. You pull it free \u2014 a fragment of parchment, torn roughly along one edge. A map fragment, by the look of it. A single curved line, maybe a road or a river, with a mark at one end like a small cross. No labels. No names. Just that one cryptic symbol.\n\nYou turn it over. Blank on the other side.\n\nYour gaze drifts to the courier's body again, and now you notice details you missed before. The cloak is torn at the shoulder \u2014 a clean slash, not frayed wear. And the small pouch at the belt has been slit open, its contents long gone. Someone searched this body before you got here. Thoroughly.\n\nThe folded letter still peeks from the snapped satchel strap. You look at it more carefully now. It's not wedged there by accident \u2014 it's been *placed*, the edge deliberately loosened so it would catch the eye of anyone who came looking.\n\nA trap? A message meant for you? A decoy left by whoever searched the pouch first?\n\nThe ash-grey dust settles around your knees. The gate looms silent above you.\n\nWhat do you do with the letter?"
