      "narrative": "The walk back to the Copper Finch is short and quiet. The crooked lane winds between shuttered buildings, their windows dark, their chimneys breathing thin ribbons of smoke into the grey sky. No one passes you. No one watches from a doorway.\n\nThe tavern's sign creaks overhead as you push the door open \u2014 a copper finch, tarnished to a dull green, nailed to a rusted iron bracket.\n\nInside, the warmth hits you first. A low fire crackles in the hearth, its light painting the scarred oak tables in amber and shadow. The air smells of old ale, woodsmoke, and beeswax. A handful of patrons sit scattered \u2014 a man nursing a cup by the window, two women in dusty travelling cloaks murmuring over a map near the far wall. No one looks up as you enter.\n\nYour old table sits against the north wall, near the hearth but not too close. The same table you left from this morning. The surface is scored with initials and ring-stains, the bench worn smooth by years of use.\n\nAs you settle into your seat, the door to the back room swings open. A figure emerges \u2014 a woman in a charcoal coat, sharp-eyed, her dark hair pulled back. Mara Venn. She carries a clay mug in one hand, steam curling from its surface.\n\nShe stops when she sees you. Her eyes flick to your empty hands, then back to your face.\n\n\"Back already,\" she says. Not a question. Her voice is flat, but her gaze is sharp \u2014 reading you, the dust on your boots, the set of your shoulders. \"You found the gate, then.\"\n\nShe doesn't sit. She waits."
